{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgx73EZ9PkCNHufnWoxMU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The flaky test is fixed and the full suite passed 10 times out of 10. It's committed as `e160d2c`.\n\n**Fix:** a one-line change at `ledger/sync.py:31`. The sort key is now `(-totals[name], name)`, so accounts with equal balances always come out alphabetically. The order no longer depends on the set's random iteration order. Nothing under `generated/` was touched.\n\n**How I tested it:** before running anything, I set the pass rule: the full suite must pass in all 10 of 10 runs. Each run of `scripts/run_tests.sh` starts a new Python process, so each one gets a new random hash seed, the same as each CI run. All 10 printed `OK`. With the old roughly 50% failure rate, 10 straight passes would happen by chance only about 1 time in 1,000.\n\n**Limits of that evidence:**\n- I didn't record which seed each run used, and I couldn't pin seeds myself because those commands need approval this session can't give. So this is strong statistical evidence that the flakiness is gone, not proof for every seed. The underlying reasoning is solid, though: with a name tie-breaker, the sort result can't depend on the order it receives the accounts.\n- I didn't run the suite before the fix, so I haven't reproduced the failure here. The evidence that it failed comes from the CI log.\n\n**Behavior change:** the display order is now fixed when balances tie, and tied accounts are listed alphabetically. That matches what the test expected, but anything that relied on the old order for ties will see a difference. Accounts with different balances are ordered exactly as before."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":268,"cache_read_input_tokens":39826,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":268},"output_tok